Date: Wed, 15 Sep 93 20:55:28 -0700
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Ethics- the virtue of

Perry Metzger recently replied with heat to Richard Ehrman's post about
gun control, and subsequently made a most generous apology, thereby
hopefully averting a flame war.
 
I was thereby prompted to reexamine the Extropian Principles, and find
that save in the title of one of the recommended readings, they do not
mention libertarianism at all, nor do they make explicit mention of many
of the issues we debate with frequent vigor.  So even if it turns out
that Ehrman should not be here, perhaps the fault is not his -- perhaps
there is a lack of available information as to what the list is about.
Perhaps more explicit information should be available to newcomers.
 
As a long-time science-fiction fan and technophile, I myself was certainly
familiar with the general ideas denoted by boundless expansion, dynamic
optimism, self-transformation, intelligent technology and spontaneous
order before I ever heard of Libertarians, much less Extropians, and if
anyone had then asked me whether those five ideas were naturally associated
with any particular political philosophy, I am sure I would have said
"don't be ridiculous", or something similar.  Perhaps other newcomers
might reasonably be forgiven for supposing, in the absence of information
to the contrary, that the future of humanity is wider than any one
specific ideological line.

                                          -- Jay Freeman

