Date: Wed, 15 Sep 93 23:10:44 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Apologies

Perry Metzger:

> Apologies to list members for the temper tantrum I just threw about
> gun control weenies posting messages to Extropians. I suspect that
> most would agree with my sentiment but not with my tone.

Here's another answer to Richard Ehrman, to give another view:

One doesn't have to love guns to get along fine here.  One
probably _does_ have to love the proposition that freedom
is a good thing and that non-coercive spontaneous orders foster
freedom and accomplishment whereas imposed orders more often
do not.  I don't control you; you don't control me -- as long
as it stays that way, we're fine.  If I were to be around people
with guns, I could choose to trust the situation, I could carry
a gun of my own, or I could try to avoid the situation.

Personally, the odds of my needing a gun where I live are
rather slim and I hardly ever even think about them.  A
gun is just a thing, and not a thing I'm personally very
interested in.  It's a genuine challenge to become good
at shooting one and I guess it's kind of fun, in the same
sort of way that bowling can be fun with the right people,
but many guns are kind of obnoxiously loud and can be used
only in limited areas and "shooting" basketballs is better
exercise.  I suppose if Somebody was going out to the
desert to try out an automatic weapon (assuming I trusted
the ability and intentions of Somebody) or something like
that, I'd go along to watch if I could bring a picnic lunch.
In general, though, they make me nervous (perhaps only through
lack of familiarity) and I'm happy enough to do without them.

You say you abhor violence; well of course!  An abhorrence
of violence is exactly where a position of non-coercion is
coming from!  It may seem like a contradiction, but it's
not.  How can we minimize violence?  Free people take
responsibility for their own protection from violence.
The whole institution of the statist police forces throws
people's view about contractual protection all out of tune.
Even to the limited extent that people are allowed to
provide for their own protection we seldom do -- at least
in part because we're trained not to think that way.
Be it crime, auto safety, or whatever, that's mommy's
job.  It's no wonder that some areas spiral out of control
(take Miami [please!])... the people there just don't pay
any attention to what's going on around them... hey, it's
not their job!

In general, because this list is centered around concepts
of freedom expansion and spontaneous order, nobody's really
very likely to want to play verbal tag on the value of
coercive freedom restrictions.  Gun control is a big topic
of debate in the U.S.  You don't have to come here to
find pro-2nd-amendment argument.  So, simply as a matter
of courtesy, it'd be best not to try to tempt people into
arguing these kind of topics.  If history is a guide, you'd
usually find somebody to rise to the bait (for a while anyway)
but nobody would be happy about it.

derek

