Date: Wed, 15 Sep 93 22:42:59 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Types of Envy

R. David  Murray writes:

> OK, if envy is 'usually associated with the darker side', can anyone
> help me improve myself a little by improving my vocabulary?  Suppose I
> have a friend who just bought a BMW, and I'd really like a BMW, but I
> can't afford one (yet).  Is there a word I can use instead of 'envy' in the
> following sentence?
> 
> "Boy, I envy you Phred!  I wish I had enough money to buy a BMW."

Recall that I explicitly used the "I envy Perry for his..." form as an
example of a non-corrosive form of "envy." We can all be envious of
others, or of their successes, their girlfriends, whatver.

"Envy" is listed in dictionaries (in contrast to "gullible," of
course), and has several senses. Several senses connote feelings of
malevolence, of bitternesss. Other senses reflect mere desire for the
things or states of others.

My point, in response to someone else's mention of "envy," was that
Nietzsche correctly observed that the bitter sense of envy can become
all-consuming and negative.

I did not make any conclusions about capitalism, about desiring
success or material wealth, etc.


> For me, it is implicit in the statement that I'm going to work on
> earning enough money to afford one (unless other priorities take
> precidence).  Resentment does not enter into it for me.  So is there a
> word for the emotion I am feeling?  Maybe my brain is out to lunch, but
> 'greed' is the only think I can think of that comes close, and there is
> no word form of it that will fit that sentence...

Nothing wrong with desiring luxuries, the good life. I don't know of a
particular word that describes "positive envy," unless it is something
like "striving" or "success-seeking." 

There's a good joke about "negative envy":

A genie appeared before a Frenchman, a Brit, and a Russian (sorry,
Sasha!). He said to them, "I will grant you each a wish."

The Frenchman said: "My neighbor has a fine vineyard, and I would like
one, too." The genie granted him his wish.

The Brit said: "My neighbor has a large manor house, and I would like
one just like it." And so it was done.

The Russian then said: "My neighbor has a fine, fat pig." The genie
interrupted and said, "And I suppose you would like one, too?"

"No, I want you to kill my neighbor's pig."


Now _that_ is resentiment!

(There are many actual studies which show that many Eastern Europeans,
brought up in a culture of "resentiment," place more importance in
ensuring that there neighbors not have more than they do, than they do
in improving their own lives. At least according to opinion polls.)

-Tim May




