If you wanted to argue in favor of superrationality the current SETI
fad is surely the poorest example!  Two centuries ago no human knew or 
cared about the hydrogen band.  Post-singularity it is likely few if any of
us will care about the hydrogen band any more than we care about
trillions of other things.  There would be no reason to look for 
posthumans in the hydrogen band rather than by looking in thousands
of other bands from long radio to hard gamma ray,
or studying interstellar clouds, or stellar physics, or taking an 
STM to asteroidal nanodiamonds, or looking for faces on Mars, or
looking for gravity waves.  Probably most postbiological action occurs with
phenomenon we haven't even discovered yet.  On a galactic timescale,
where if only a million years pass between the birth of civilizations
the galaxy should be full of them,  the chance of any aliens being 
at our level of civilization and caring about the same things our 
scientists do (eg the hydrogen band wanting to contact highly speculative
ETI), rather than being pre-radio or post-singularity and caring about 
very different things, are so minute they can be ignored.  The current 
NASA obsession with this One True Way to Look for Aliens is so provincial 
it's scary.  But then, the NASA and NASA groupie mindset in general is
so provincial it's scary.  Don't get me started!

